The mathematical component is about graphing the function y = 2x - 1. The animation component involves using a "Rise/Run" or "slope" animation to illustrate that the slope of this line is 2.

<reflection>
Before proceeding, let's ensure we understand the mathematical concept and the animation technique involved. The function y = 2x - 1 represents a linear equation in slope-intercept form, y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept. In this case, the slope (m) is 2, and the y-intercept (b) is -1. The "Rise/Run" animation technique is used to visually demonstrate the slope of a line by showing how much the line rises (vertically) over a given run (horizontally). For a line with a slope of 2, this means for every one unit the line runs to the right, it rises two units upwards.
</reflection>

<final_translation>
Create an animation that graphs the function y = 2x - 1. Utilize a "Rise/Run" or slope animation to demonstrate that the slope of this line is 2, explicitly showing how for every one unit of horizontal movement (run), the line moves two units upwards (rise), thereby visually representing the slope.
</final_translation>
